(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-misc-bacterial-agents-quiz"]={"title":"Miscellaneous Bacterial Agents — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which vibrio causes gastroenteritis from raw oysters, with serious complications in people with diabetes or liver disease?","choices":["Vibrio cholerae","Campylobacter jejuni","Vibrio vulnificus","Vibrio parahaemolyticus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Vibrio vulnificus causes gastroenteritis from raw oysters, with serious complications in persons with diabetes or liver disease; it is one of the \"flesh-eating\" bacteria. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which spirochete genus is especially noted for linear double-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid and plasmids?","choices":["Vibrio","Borrelia","Treponema","Leptospira"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: most bacteria have circular chromosomes, but these narrow cells may carry linear double-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid and plasmids, especially Borrelia. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which organism causes Rocky Mountain spotted fever?","choices":["Borrelia burgdorferi","Rickettsia rickettsii","Rickettsia typhi","Rickettsia akari"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Rocky Mountain spotted fever is a Rickettsia rickettsii zoonosis carried by dog and wood ticks, with a distinct spotted rash. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 46 and image on Slide 47"},{"q":"How do spirochetes such as Treponema, Leptospira and Borrelia move?","choices":["By one polar flagellum outside","By pili that pull the cell","They are nonmotile","By endoflagella inside the cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: spirochetes are motile by means of endoflagella, which are found inside the cell, in the periplasmic space beneath the outer membrane. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which ticks transmit Lyme disease?","choices":["Ixodes ticks","Dog and wood ticks","Body lice","Rat fleas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Borrelia burgdorferi is carried by the white-footed mouse and transmitted by Ixodes ticks. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which diseases is Helicobacter pylori linked to?","choices":["Dental caries","Ulcers and gastric cancer","Secretory diarrhea","Atypical pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Helicobacter pylori causes most stomach and duodenal ulcers and is an apparent cofactor in gastric cancer. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the reservoir of Chlamydia trachomatis?","choices":["Birds","Rodents","Cattle","Humans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Chlamydia trachomatis has a human reservoir; its two strains cause trachoma and lymphogranuloma venereum. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which disease does Ehrlichia chaffeensis cause?","choices":["Epidemic louse-borne typhus","Mite-borne rickettsialpox","Human monocytic ehrlichiosis","Rocky Mountain spotted fever"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Ehrlichia chaffeensis causes human monocytic ehrlichiosis, a tick-borne infection whose distribution is similar to that of Rocky Mountain spotted fever. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 46 and image on Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the bull's-eye rash at the site of the tick bite in Lyme disease called?","choices":["Erythema migrans","A gumma","A hard chancre","Bacillary angiomatosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: erythema migrans is the bull's-eye rash at the initial tick-bite location in Lyme disease. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 26 and 30"},{"q":"Which chlamydial form is the infectious one released by the infected host cell?","choices":["The reticulate body","The endospore","The inclusion body","The elementary body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the elementary body is small, metabolically inactive, extracellular and infectious, released by the infected host cell; the reticulate body is the noninfectious dividing form. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 56 and image on Slide 57"},{"q":"Which description fits Rickettsia?","choices":["Wall-less, highly variable cells","Comma rods with one flagellum","Tiny, nonmotile Gram-negative rods","Large spirals with endoflagella"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: rickettsias are obligate intracellular parasites with a Gram-negative wall, among the smallest bacteria, nonmotile pleomorphic rods or coccobacilli. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 42 and 44"},{"q":"Which organs does Leptospira interrogans target?","choices":["Kidneys, liver, brain and eyes","Stomach and duodenum","Lining of small blood vessels","Gums and tooth sockets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: leptospirosis targets the kidneys, liver, brain and eyes, and long-term infections may affect the kidneys and liver. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why might erythromycin or tetracycline be used to treat syphilis?","choices":["They work better than penicillin","The disease is tertiary","The patient is pregnant","Penicillin is contraindicated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: erythromycin and tetracycline are less effective than penicillin G or doxycycline but can be used when penicillin is contraindicated, for example by patient allergy. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which group is at highest risk of Q fever?","choices":["Hikers in tick country","Farmers and veterinarians","Surfers and water-skiers","Owners of pet cats"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a Q fever vaccine is available, and veterinarians, farmers and lab technicians are at highest risk; historic cases most likely arose from contaminated milking barns. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In relapsing fever, what brings each bout of high fever back down?","choices":["The tick detaching","Loss of the endoflagella","An antibody response","Tetracycline taken by mouth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the initial antibody response at first reduces the fever, until a mutant Borrelia with a new antigen causes a relapse, and each new antibody response again reduces the symptoms. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 24 and image on Slide 25"},{"q":"What does cholera toxin cause?","choices":["Necrosis of vessel linings","Secretory, rice water diarrhea","Bloody diarrhea from invasion","Ulcers of the stomach lining"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: cholera toxin causes electrolyte and water loss through secretory diarrhea, \"rice water stool\", and the dehydration leads to muscle, circulatory and neurological symptoms. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does a fetus acquire syphilis?","choices":["From a tick bite in infancy","From the mother's urine","Across the placenta","Through the birth canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: congenital syphilis is a trans-placental infection of the fetus, causing nasal discharge, skin eruptions, bone deformation and nervous system abnormalities after birth. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 6 and 10"},{"q":"What do mycoplasmas require from host cell membranes?","choices":["Adenosine triphosphate","Urea for urease","Peptidoglycan","Special lipids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: mycoplasmas naturally lack cell walls, are highly pleomorphic and require special lipids from host membranes. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How do newly hatched tick larvae become infected with Borrelia burgdorferi?","choices":["By feeding on infected mice","By hatching from infected eggs","By feeding on deer","By biting infected humans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: newly hatched larvae become infected when they feed on small animals such as mice, which harbor the spirochete, and continue development through the first year. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 26 and image on Slide 28"},{"q":"In veterinary medicine, how is Campylobacter fetus spread among domestic herd animals?","choices":["As a sexually transmitted disease","By tick bites in pastures","By fleas from rodents","By airborne barn particles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Campylobacter fetus is a significant veterinary pathogen as a sexually transmitted disease of domestic herd animals, passed animal to animal in cattle, sheep and goats; how humans acquire it remains uncertain. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which organism causes relapsing fever?","choices":["Borrelia burgdorferi","Rickettsia typhi","Leptospira interrogans","Borrelia hermsii"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Borrelia hermsii causes relapsing fever, with recurrent febrile and afebrile cycles; Borrelia burgdorferi causes Lyme disease. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 23 and 24"},{"q":"How does a newborn acquire chlamydial inclusion conjunctivitis?","choices":["Via the birth canal","Across the placenta","From flies after birth","From a pet bird at home"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: inclusion conjunctivitis occurs as the baby passes through the birth canal, and it is prevented by prophylaxis. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 58 and image on Slide 59"},{"q":"Which nonsyphilitic treponematosis is a superficial skin lesion that depigments and scars the skin?","choices":["Yaws","Bejel","Relapsing fever","Pinta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: pinta, caused by Treponema carateum, is a superficial skin lesion that depigments and scars the skin. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does dental plaque damage tooth enamel?","choices":["It blocks the tooth's blood","Its bacteria make acid","Its bacteria make urease","It hardens into the gums"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: plaque harbors masses of bacteria that produce acid, which dissolves the enamel, leading to dental caries. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which eye sign of tertiary syphilis eventually ends in loss of the light reflex?","choices":["Inclusion conjunctivitis","Leptospiral conjunctivitis","Argyll Robertson pupil","Ocular trachoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the Argyll Robertson pupil of tertiary syphilis is irregular in shape and eventually loses its light reflex. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 14 and image on Slide 14"},{"q":"In the progression of dental disease, which step leads toward gingivitis rather than toward caries?","choices":["Calculus formation","Acidification and cavitation","Pellicle formation","Damage to the enamel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: after plaque forms, one branch is acidification and cavitation leading to caries; the other is calculus (tartar) formation, leading to gingivitis, periodontal disease and bone resorption. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 67 and image on Slide 66"},{"q":"Why can Vibrio cholerae need a very large infectious dose?","choices":["It must first multiply in urine","It must first form a spore","It must first pass the stomach","It must first enter the blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the infectious dose can run very high because the vibrios must first pass through the stomach to infect the intestines. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What do rickettsias need in order to be cultivated?","choices":["Live host cells","Blood agar alone","Host-membrane lipids","A salt-rich medium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: rickettsias are very small intracellular parasites that require live cells for cultivation. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which lesions develop in tertiary syphilis?","choices":["Hutchinson's teeth","Gummas","Hard chancres","Palm and sole rashes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: tertiary syphilis can last 20 years or longer; neurological and cardiovascular symptoms develop and gummas form in organs. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 10 and 14"},{"q":"Helicobacter pylori's urease converts urea into which products?","choices":["Glucose and nitrogen","Ammonium and bicarbonate","Lactic acid and water","Hydrochloric acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: urease converts urea into ammonium and bicarbonate, which raises the local pH and lets the bacterium survive in the stomach. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
